  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nUpper ridging will continue to bring very warm temperatures to the\nregion through the weekend. Isolated triple digit heat is possible\nin the Yellowstone corridor from Terry to Sidney. The heat could\nbring Advisory effects Saturday with the added humidity.\n\nWeak embedded shortwaves will continue to tap deep monsoonal\nmoisture to offer daily chances for thunderstorms. High\nprecipitable water values will mean any given storm could be a\nprolific rainfall producer. In addition, the SPC has included the\nwestern zones of Phillips and Petroleum Counties for today in a\nmarginal risk for severe thunderstorms but the latest CAMS are\nshowing low chances for our CWA, so could be a dry night.\n\nOff and on chances for storms will continue through Sunday but we\ncould be too capped for anything to fire up. In other words,\nconfidence is quite low that anything will happen.\n\nNighttime fog chances will be low but winds might be too strong\nfor it's formation.\n\nOn Monday, an upper low will drop southeast into south-central\nCanada. This will bring a breezy day so a Lake Wind Advisory will\nlikely be needed. But it will feel refreshing for most.\n\nThe upper ridge returns next week and that will allow temps to\nrebound back into the mid 80s to mid 90s by the end of the week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nSome massaging of pops were made to try to reflect what the CAMS\nmodels were showing through Saturday morning. TFJ",
